1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:05,141
The hotel in the middle of Zion
Canyon sits on the bottom of a

2
00:00:05,181 --> 00:00:08,720
lake, a lake that vanished
more than 4,000 years ago.

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:17,090
Where this lawn is now, the water once
stood about 350 to 380 feet deep.

4
00:00:18,930 --> 00:00:20,910
And that lake was made in about a minute.

5
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:29,497
About 4,800 years ago, a huge part
of the canyon wall near a peak

6
00:00:29,537 --> 00:00:33,805
called the Sentinel broke loose
and fell into Zion Canyon.

7
00:00:33,845 --> 00:00:38,130
Models suggest it reached
nearly 200 miles per hour.

8
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:40,940
One of the geologists
who studied it put it

9
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:43,920
simply: by a minute, it
was pretty much done.

10
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,520
The rubble dammed the
Virgin River, and behind

11
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:50,990
it grew a lake that some call Lake Zion.

12
00:00:51,030 --> 00:00:53,925
It lasted about 700 years.

13
00:00:53,965 --> 00:00:58,881
When it finally filled with sand and
mud, it left the flat green valley

14
00:00:58,921 --> 00:01:03,110
floor that millions of visitors
now walk, ride and sleep on.

15
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:10,110
The same geologist called it a minute
with up to 10,000 years of consequences.

16
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:15,270
And the mountain isn't finished.

17
00:01:15,310 --> 00:01:19,504
In 1995, part of that
ancient slide moved again,

18
00:01:19,544 --> 00:01:23,690
and about 430 people were
cut off at the lodge.

19
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:28,610
This is America's National
Parks: The Hidden History.

20
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:30,285
Zion, Part Two.

21
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:31,900
The Day the Mountain Fell.

22
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:47,855
The walls of Zion Canyon rise 2,000
to 3,000 feet above the canyon floor.

23
00:01:47,895 --> 00:01:52,540
The Park Service counts them among the
highest sandstone cliffs on Earth.

24
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:58,098
But in the middle of the canyon,
the floor goes strangely

25
00:01:58,138 --> 00:02:01,395
flat, wide enough for
meadows, a road and a hotel.

26
00:02:01,435 --> 00:02:06,990
In 2016, a team from the University
of Utah published the explanation.

27
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:13,054
They dated the slide using
beryllium-ten, a rare form of the

28
00:02:13,094 --> 00:02:17,390
element that slowly builds up
in rock exposed to cosmic rays.

29
00:02:17,430 --> 00:02:22,038
Measured in boulders the
slide left behind, it gave

30
00:02:22,078 --> 00:02:26,270
an age of about 4,800
years, give or take 400.

31
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,535
What fell was enormous: about
10 billion cubic feet of rock.

32
00:02:32,575 --> 00:02:36,566
To rebuild that missing
mountainside, the researchers said,

33
00:02:36,606 --> 00:02:40,090
you would need 90 times the
concrete in Hoover Dam.

34
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:45,906
It was roughly 5 times bigger
than the largest landslides in

35
00:02:45,946 --> 00:02:49,780
North America's recorded
history, not counting volcanoes.

36
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:55,815
Models suggest it averaged
about 112 miles per hour.

37
00:02:55,855 --> 00:02:59,780
The main mass crossed the
canyon in about 20 seconds.

38
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:02,580
The debris kept spreading
for about 30 more.

39
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:09,318
When the dust settled, the canyon
was blocked from wall to wall by a

40
00:03:09,358 --> 00:03:13,760
dam about 2 miles long and, in
places, about 650 feet thick.

41
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:23,375
The Virgin River had nowhere to go.

42
00:03:23,415 --> 00:03:27,510
It backed up behind the rubble,
and the canyon began to fill.

43
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:30,493
The researchers estimate
the lake reached its

44
00:03:30,533 --> 00:03:33,450
first high point within
about 5 to 10 years.

45
00:03:35,070 --> 00:03:36,950
Picture this view as water.

46
00:03:36,990 --> 00:03:39,929
At its highest, the
lake covered almost 2.5

47
00:03:39,969 --> 00:03:43,185
square miles and reached
almost to the Narrows.

48
00:03:43,225 --> 00:03:46,878
Over the spot where Zion Lodge stands

49
00:03:46,918 --> 00:03:51,010
today, it was about 350 to 380 feet deep.

50
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:55,290
Then the water found a way over the top.

51
00:03:55,330 --> 00:04:00,220
The lake cut into the crest of its dam
and dropped to a lower, steadier level.

52
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:04,961
It shrank to about 1.2 square
miles, but it still reached more

53
00:04:05,001 --> 00:04:09,130
than 4 miles up the canyon,
to the Temple of Sinawava.

54
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:14,195
And then, slowly, the
river buried its own lake.

55
00:04:14,235 --> 00:04:17,099
Year after year it carried
sand and mud down the

56
00:04:17,139 --> 00:04:19,740
canyon and dropped them
in the still water.

57
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:28,220
About 700 years after the mountain fell,
around 4,100 years ago, the lake was full.

58
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:30,380
Not of water. Of sediment.

59
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:34,780
That is the flat floor of Zion Canyon.

60
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:38,372
The meadows, the cottonwoods,
the road and the lodge

61
00:04:38,412 --> 00:04:41,480
lawn all sit on a lake
that filled itself in.

62
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:45,912
Jeff Moore, one of the University
of Utah geologists behind the

63
00:04:45,952 --> 00:04:49,680
study, called it creating
paradise through cataclysm.

64
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:54,960
The river has been eating
away at the dam ever since.

65
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,261
The researchers estimate that
about 45 percent of the slide's

66
00:04:59,301 --> 00:05:03,250
debris is already gone, carried
off by the Virgin River.

67
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:14,120
Thousands of years later, people
found that flat floor useful.

68
00:05:15,830 --> 00:05:20,151
In 1917, the first road for
cars reached deep into Zion

69
00:05:20,191 --> 00:05:24,115
Canyon, and a tent camp
opened on the old lakebed.

70
00:05:24,155 --> 00:05:28,420
It had 10 tent cabins, right
where the lodge stands today.

71
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:35,535
In the early 1920s, the Union Pacific
Railroad saw a business opportunity.

72
00:05:35,575 --> 00:05:40,079
A rail spur reached Cedar
City, Utah, in 1923, and the

73
00:05:40,119 --> 00:05:45,380
railroad's Utah Parks Company
needed a hotel inside the canyon.

74
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:48,800
The company hired Gilbert
Stanley Underwood, an

75
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:52,875
architect who designed lodges
in several national parks.

76
00:05:52,915 --> 00:05:56,250
Zion Lodge was finished in May 1925.

77
00:05:58,100 --> 00:05:59,870
Here's the detail we love.

78
00:05:59,910 --> 00:06:03,738
The lodge was built with
265,000 board feet of

79
00:06:03,778 --> 00:06:07,775
lumber, brought down from
the plateau on a wire.

80
00:06:07,815 --> 00:06:12,052
A tramway called the Cable Mountain
Draw Works lowered it more

81
00:06:12,092 --> 00:06:16,300
than 2,000 vertical feet, down
the cliff to the canyon floor.

82
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:21,570
Hold on to that name, Cable
Mountain. We'll come back to it.

83
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:27,920
For more than 40 years, Underwood's
lodge stood on the lakebed.

84
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:33,530
Then, on January 26, 1966, the
main lodge building burned.

85
00:06:35,390 --> 00:06:37,290
The replacement went up fast.

86
00:06:37,330 --> 00:06:43,010
A new, prefabricated building was
operating just 108 days after the fire.

87
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:50,595
In 1990, the lodge was restored to look
more like Underwood's original design.

88
00:06:50,635 --> 00:06:55,010
That's the lodge you see today,
standing in the middle of the old lake.

89
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:59,660
And in 1995, it became an island.

90
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:12,890
On the night of April 12, 1995, part
of the old Sentinel slide moved again.

91
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:19,794
A landslide more than 500 feet
long, over 100,000 cubic yards of

92
00:07:19,834 --> 00:07:24,330
rock and soil, slid into the
Virgin River and blocked it.

93
00:07:24,370 --> 00:07:27,310
A small lake began to form behind it.

94
00:07:27,350 --> 00:07:32,100
And the river, pushed out of its bed, cut
a new path straight through the road.

95
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:38,035
Rangers estimated that 600
feet of road was destroyed.

96
00:07:38,075 --> 00:07:41,305
It was the only road in
and out of the canyon.

97
00:07:41,345 --> 00:07:45,772
Upstream, at Zion Lodge,
about 430 people, guests and

98
00:07:45,812 --> 00:07:50,280
employees, by the Park
Service's count, were cut off.

99
00:07:52,150 --> 00:07:56,880
Crews carved an emergency detour
road out of the canyon's east wall.

100
00:07:56,920 --> 00:07:58,910
It took about 22 hours.

101
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:04,630
On the night of April 13, the people stuck
at the lodge were finally able to leave.

102
00:08:06,490 --> 00:08:09,500
Three years later, the
old slide was hit again.

103
00:08:09,540 --> 00:08:15,435
In 1998, a flash flood sent the
river from 200 to 4,500 cubic feet

104
00:08:15,475 --> 00:08:20,600
per second, and it damaged the
scenic drive at the slide.

105
00:08:22,430 --> 00:08:25,465
The dam from 4,800 years
ago is still there,

106
00:08:25,505 --> 00:08:28,230
and the river is still cutting into it.

107
00:08:35,510 --> 00:08:37,450
The Park Service puts it plainly.

108
00:08:37,490 --> 00:08:41,234
In Zion, rocks fall, frequently,
and it is impossible to

109
00:08:41,274 --> 00:08:44,450
predict when or where the
next one will happen.

110
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:49,535
Water seeps into cracks and
freezes, wedging them apart.

111
00:08:49,575 --> 00:08:52,687
The sun heats the rock
by day and it cools at

112
00:08:52,727 --> 00:08:56,190
night, expanding and
shrinking until it fractures.

113
00:08:56,230 --> 00:08:58,795
Floods undercut the cliffs from below.

114
00:08:58,835 --> 00:09:03,030
And sometimes, as the park
itself says, rocks just fall.

115
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:09,503
On August 1, 1968, about 5,000
tons of rock came down at

116
00:09:09,543 --> 00:09:13,490
the end of the trail that
leads to the Narrows.

117
00:09:15,170 --> 00:09:20,581
Then, on the evening of August 24,
2019, a huge slab of sandstone

118
00:09:20,621 --> 00:09:25,780
broke off Cable Mountain, about
3,000 feet above Weeping Rock.

119
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:30,940
Yes, the same Cable Mountain that once
lowered the lumber for the lodge.

120
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:37,615
More than 435,000 cubic
feet of debris came down.

121
00:09:37,655 --> 00:09:41,860
Three visitors were hurt, and
one was taken to the hospital.

122
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:45,275
Part of the East Rim Trail
was buried under what

123
00:09:45,315 --> 00:09:48,730
geologists classified as
a small rock avalanche.

124
00:09:50,350 --> 00:09:53,925
The Weeping Rock Trail
stayed closed for 3 years.

125
00:09:53,965 --> 00:09:56,840
It reopened in November 2022.

126
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:02,810
A year later, another slab fell near the
same spot, and the trail closed again.

127
00:10:02,850 --> 00:10:05,970
It didn't reopen until September 2025.

128
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:12,613
After that second fall, the park's
superintendent, Jeff Bradybaugh, said it

129
00:10:12,653 --> 00:10:17,210
well: rockfall can happen at any time
in this highly erosive landscape.

130
00:10:19,010 --> 00:10:23,720
And yet, according to the Park Service,
no visitor has ever been killed by a

131
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:28,760
rockfall in Zion, though people have been
hurt, and there have been close calls.

132
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:39,535
Today, mule deer graze in the
meadows on the old lakebed.

133
00:10:39,575 --> 00:10:43,148
But two Oregon State
University ecologists argue

134
00:10:43,188 --> 00:10:46,800
that even this quiet scene
has a hidden history.

135
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:54,425
By their count, in 1924, about
8,400 people visited Zion.

136
00:10:54,465 --> 00:10:58,795
Ten years later, almost 69,000 did.

137
00:10:58,835 --> 00:11:04,680
A Park Service report from 1939 said the
crowds tended to drive out the cougars.

138
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:11,049
Without cougars, the researchers
say, the canyon's deer

139
00:11:11,089 --> 00:11:15,925
boomed, from fewer than 80 in
1930 to about 600 by 1942.

140
00:11:15,965 --> 00:11:20,161
Between 1938 and 1947, the Park Service

141
00:11:20,201 --> 00:11:24,980
killed or removed 780
deer from Zion Canyon.

142
00:11:26,830 --> 00:11:28,995
And the deer ate the young cottonwoods.

143
00:11:29,035 --> 00:11:33,792
In Zion Canyon, the researchers
found only about 23 cottonwoods

144
00:11:33,832 --> 00:11:37,980
per kilometer of stream that
had taken root after 1940.

145
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:43,535
In nearby North Creek, where the
cougars stayed, there were almost 900.

146
00:11:43,575 --> 00:11:47,400
Without new trees holding the
soil, the banks wore away.

147
00:11:49,210 --> 00:11:55,130
It is the authors' hypothesis, and ideas
like this are debated among ecologists.

148
00:11:55,170 --> 00:11:58,590
But it's a reminder that the
lakebed is still changing too.

149
00:12:00,310 --> 00:12:04,455
So the next time you stand on the
lawn at Zion Lodge, look down.

150
00:12:04,495 --> 00:12:08,477
You are standing on the mud and
sand of a lake that some call Lake

151
00:12:08,517 --> 00:12:12,540
Zion, a lake born from a mountainside
that fell in about a minute.

152
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:18,526
Could it happen again? Jeff Moore
chose his words carefully: we have no

153
00:12:18,566 --> 00:12:21,350
evidence that something
like this is imminent.

154
00:12:21,390 --> 00:12:25,890
But this canyon is still being
built, one falling rock at a time.

155
00:12:27,730 --> 00:12:31,810
Building anything in this canyon has
always meant a fight with the rock.

156
00:12:31,850 --> 00:12:34,565
And the boldest fight of all was a road.

157
00:12:34,605 --> 00:12:38,210
In the late 1920s, crews
set out to blast a tunnel

158
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:41,750
more than a mile long
through a sandstone cliff.

159
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:46,438
How they did it, and how
this canyon became a

160
00:12:46,478 --> 00:12:49,200
national park at all,
is our final chapter.

161
00:12:50,910 --> 00:12:51,910
See you there.

